WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.470
In nineteen forty-seven, George

2
00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:04.770
Dantzig wanted to plan military

3
00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:07.010
supplies at the least cost.

4
00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:10.710
His method now plans airline crews,

5
00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:15.030
refineries and hospital meals, and

6
00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:16.650
it runs on your phone.

7
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.380
Let’s see why it works.

8
00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:23.590
Today: linear programming, and how the

9
00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:26.130
simplex method finds the best corner.

10
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:29.540
A linear program has continuous

11
00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:33.060
variables, linear limits and a linear

12
00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:33.420
goal.

13
00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:37.790
In standard form: maximise c times x,

14
00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:43.190
subject to A x at most b, and x at least

15
00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:43.530
zero.

16
00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.380
The school garden is one: thirty

17
00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:49.740
x plus forty y, under limits on

18
00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:51.520
land, water and hours.

19
00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:54.990
Each limit cuts the plane in half,

20
00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:57.210
and the plans that keep them all

21
00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:58.990
form a convex polygon.

22
00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:01.550
A linear goal is a family

23
00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:02.890
of parallel lines.

24
00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:06.570
Slide it as far as you can, and the

25
00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:08.670
last point it touches is a corner.

26
00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:12.430
Simplex walks from corner to neighbouring

27
00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:15.390
corner, improving the goal each time, and

28
00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:17.190
stops when no neighbour is better.

29
00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.460
Because the polygon is convex, that

30
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:21.880
corner is the best of all.

31
00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:25.680
In the garden: from the origin to six bean

32
00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.840
beds, then to three tomato and five bean

33
00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.680
beds, a profit of two hundred and ninety.

34
00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:35.170
In the worst case, simplex visits

35
00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:38.030
exponentially many corners, but

36
00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:39.410
in practice it’s fast.

37
00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:42.250
Interior-point methods cut through

38
00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:44.390
the middle, in polynomial time.

39
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.100
GLOP, in Google’s OR-Tools,

40
00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.020
is a simplex solver.

41
00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:50.750
Quick check!

42
00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:54.780
A linear program’s best value is

43
00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:57.400
reached at two neighbouring corners.

44
00:01:58.270 --> 00:01:59.450
What does that tell you?

45
00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:02.000
Every point on the edge

46
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.760
between them is optimal too.

47
00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:07.490
Now the second big idea: every linear

48
00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:10.470
program has a twin, its dual, whose

49
00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.350
variables are prices for the limits.

50
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.220
A limit’s shadow price is how

51
00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:17.400
much the best goal improves if

52
00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.920
that limit grows by one.

53
00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.970
In the garden, one more pupil-hour is

54
00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:25.570
worth five, one more bed of land

55
00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:28.590
twenty-five, and water nothing,

56
00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:30.630
because three cans are spare.

57
00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:33.980
That’s complementary slackness: a limit

58
00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:36.600
with room to spare has a price of zero.

59
00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.060
Solvers report these dual

60
00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:42.260
values: in OR-Tools, ask each

61
00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:43.980
constraint for its dual value.

62
00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:46.750
Solving again with the limit one

63
00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:48.870
larger gives the same five.

64
00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:52.150
The diet problem was one of the

65
00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:53.550
first linear programs.

66
00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:57.310
The cheapest school lunch costs forty-one

67
00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:00.330
coins, with energy and iron met exactly.

68
00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:02.930
Protein comes free, so its

69
00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:04.230
shadow price is zero.

70
00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.710
In OR-Tools, the whole

71
00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:09.250
model is a few lines.

72
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.780
Make a solver, add the variables and the

73
00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:16.240
limits, set the goal, and GLOP solves it.

74
00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.840
The same program runs unchanged in Colab.

75
00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:22.970
In the app, build a school lunch,

76
00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:24.870
then press find the cheapest.

77
00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:27.520
Read the corners the simplex method

78
00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:30.780
visited, and which needs are met exactly.

79
00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:32.840
Your turn.

80
00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.800
In the cheapest lunch, protein comes to

81
00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:40.020
twenty-three grams against a need of

82
00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:40.560
twenty.

83
00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:44.260
What is protein’s shadow price?

84
00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:47.810
Zero: a limit with room to

85
00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:49.330
spare costs nothing.

86
00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.020
Remember: the feasible set is convex,

87
00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:55.380
and the best is at a corner.

88
00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:58.600
Simplex walks from corner to corner.

89
00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.300
And shadow prices tell you

90
00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:02.580
what each limit is worth.

91
00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:06.100
Try it yourself: write the garden

92
00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.420
as a linear program in GLOP.

93
00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:12.410
Then add one hour, solve again, and

94
00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:15.070
check that the profit rises by five.
